"Summarizes how program resources and activities are expected to produce direct outputs and progressively longer-term outcomes.\n\nUse when: Align implementation and evaluation around a bounded program. Distinguish delivery counts from intended changes in participants or conditions.\n\nAvoid when: The central question requires detailed alternative causal pathways and contested assumptions.\n\nVisual grammar: Ordered regions distinguish inputs, activities, outputs and outcome horizons. Connections express the program's proposed contribution logic. Context and assumption notes qualify the main chain.\n\nBuild: Define the program's target population and intended outcomes. Identify activities and the resources needed to deliver them. Specify observable outputs and near-, medium- and long-term outcome indicators.\n\nCheck: Outputs count delivered work while outcomes describe resulting change. Resources and activities plausibly support the stated delivery. Outcome measures match the relevant horizon and population.\n\nPitfalls: Calling attendance totals an outcome without describing any change. Assuming a tidy chain establishes causation or attribution.\n\nExample: A community workshop links staff and materials to sessions, attendance, learned skills and later use of those skills at home.\n\nAlternatives: Theory of change, Impact map."
